    think:{quote:"As Magwitch lies dying in the prison hospital, Pip tells him that the daughter he believed lost for ever is alive and that Pip himself loves her, and a faint, grateful smile crosses the old man's face as he dies believing himself fortunate.", question:"Magwitch dies in prison, having lost his fortune and his freedom, yet Pip describes him as dying feeling fortunate. What does this suggest about what really matters to Magwitch at the end of his life?", options:["Money and freedom matter more to him than anything else","Being loved, and knowing his daughter is alive and loved too, matters more to him than money or freedom","He does not understand what is happening to him"], answer:1, note:"Magwitch's whole life has been driven by love for Pip and, unknowingly, for his lost daughter; dying with both loves finally acknowledged is, for him, a kind of happiness money could never have bought."},
